* {{rhymes|en|ɪfθɔŋ|ɪfθɑŋ|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{examples|sense=phonetics|* "ou" in {{m|en|loud}}
* "i" in {{m|en|mine}}
}}
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|phonetics}} A complex [[vowel]] [[sound]] that begins with the sound of one vowel and ends with the sound of another vowel, in the same [[syllable]].
#: {{syn|en|gliding vowel}}
#: {{coordinate terms|en|monophthong|triphthong}}
# {{lb|en|rare}} A vowel [[digraph]] or [[ligature]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1854|author=Robert Bigsby|title=Historical and Topographical Description of Repton, in the County of Derby|publisher=Woodfall and Kinder|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=68oHAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA47&dq=diphthong|page=47|passage=And he might have written the name, also, with the '''diphthong''' æ, as well as the single vowel, in the initial syllable, throughout all the preceding forms.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1860|author=Joseph E. Worcester|title=An Elementary Dictionary of the English Language|publisher=Swan, Brewer, and Tileston|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=CuIzKfd61ZcC&pg=PA12&dq=diphthong|page=12|passage=An improper '''diphthong''' has only one of the vowels sounded; as, ''ea'' in ''heat'', ''oa'' in ''coal''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1874|author=Theophilus Dwight Hall|title=A Child’s First Latin Book|publisher=John Murray|url=http://books.google.com/books?id=rsMDAAAAQAAJ&pg=PA3&dq=diphthong|page=3|text=The '''diphthong''' ''ae'' is sounded like ''ē'' (§7); that is, it has the sound of ''ey'' in ''they''.}}

